{-
   Where the stream goes, natively - Design-Test.md §5.2.

   **Descriptor 3 when it is open, and standard error otherwise.** Both halves of that matter. A
   harness that spawned this process opens 3 for it and gets the report on a channel of its own, so
   a case's own `print` and the runner's report never interleave into one stream that then has to be
   picked apart. A binary run by hand has no 3, and the report is still worth having - stderr rather
   than stdout, so that a run whose output is being read for something else still says what it did.

   Whether 3 is open is asked with a **zero-length write**, which is the one question this can ask
   without an `fcntl` the library does not have: the kernel checks the descriptor before it checks
   the count, so an open 3 answers 0 and a closed one answers `EBADF`. Asked once and remembered,
   because it is a property of the process rather than of the line.

   Written through `File` rather than through `writeFile` directly, so that the short-write loop is
   the one `File` already has an opinion about and the byte pointer of a `String` stays inside the
   module that owns that question - see the note above `bytesOf` in File.yana.
-}

import File

-- 0 for "not asked yet", which is the one descriptor this can never answer: a runner whose report
-- went to standard input would be writing into what it reads. Constant, so this is an initializer a
-- library module's global may have.
let &stream = 0 :: Size

fn reportStream() -> Size:
    if stream != (0 :: Size) then return stream

    -- The probe. A count of zero writes nothing on a descriptor that is open and reports on one
    -- that is not, so this costs one syscall and never disturbs the stream it is asking about.
    let &probe = borrowFile(3)
    stream = if probe.writeText("") is Ok(_) then 3 else 2
    return stream

{-
   Whether the stream has a channel of its own.

   True when descriptor 3 was open, which means something *started* this process to read its report -
   and that is the whole of what the runner needs to decide whether to print anything for a human as
   well. A child says nothing; the parent that is reading the stream is what reports. A binary run at
   a terminal has no 3, prints its failures and its count, and still writes the stream to standard
   error so that a crash is attributable.
-}
pub fn reportIsPrivate() -> Bool = reportStream() == (3 :: Size)

-- Whether the stream lands where `print` does. Never, here: the stream is descriptor 3 or standard
-- error and `print` is standard output, so the two reports of §5.5 cannot interleave on this target
-- however the process was started. The JavaScript half has one output and answers otherwise.
pub fn reportSharesOutput() -> Bool = False

-- One line, unbuffered and on its own descriptor. A failed write is dropped rather than reported:
-- there is nowhere left to report it to, and a runner that stopped the suite because its own report
-- could not be delivered would turn a closed pipe into a failed test run.
pub fn reportLine(text: String) -> {}:
    if reportMuted() then return

    let &out = borrowFile(reportStream())
    out.writeText(text)
